<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Kuna devirtualize, the frame of a recovered body: P6 variables, option
    deobfuscate (default auto).  The script loads the x86-64 PIE
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/devirt_frame_x86_64
    (source devirt_frame_x86_64.c, gcc -O0).  tg_between is its own bytecode
    interpreter in Tigress's style, running

        st[0..3] = a, a + 1, a + 2, a + 3;
        s = 0;
        for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            s += *(long *)((char *)&st[0] + i * 8);
            st[2] = s;
            s += *(long *)((char *)&st[0] + i * 8);
        }
        return s;

    The first indexed read has two uses, on both sides of the write of
    st[2]: the sum stored there and the sum carried on.  It printed inside
    both, so the one after the write read st[2] again and for i = 2 took
    the value just stored: `v1[2] = v1[v3] + v2; v2 = v1[v3] + v1[v3] + v2;`.
    A read through a computed frame address is now printed ahead of every
    direct write of frame storage it may read, so it keeps its own
    statement (#2, #3, #4).
-->
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/devirt_frame_x86_64</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function tg_between</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="DEVIRTUALIZE-FRAME #1: tg_between is devirtualized" min="1" max="1">devirtualize: VM-protected function recovered</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DEVIRTUALIZE-FRAME #2: the read before the write of st[2] is one statement" min="1" max="1">^\s*v[0-9]+ = v[0-9]+\[v[0-9]+\];</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DEVIRTUALIZE-FRAME #4: the write of st[2] uses the value read" min="1" max="1">v[0-9]+\[2\] = v[0-9]+ \+ v[0-9]+;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DEVIRTUALIZE-FRAME #3: the read is not printed again after the write" min="0" max="0">v[0-9]+\[v[0-9]+\] \+ v[0-9]+\[v[0-9]+\]</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
